.TH SHARDPOSTCTL-DELIVER 1 "2026-10-01" "ShardPost 0.35.2" "ShardPost Manual"
.SH NAME
shardpostctl-deliver \- deliver a message like a local delivery agent
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B shardpostctl deliver
.BI \-d " recipient"
.RB [ \-f
.IR sender ]
.RB [ \-m
.IR mailbox ]
.RB [ \-\-no-sieve ]
.RB [ \-\-no-enforce-quota ]
.RB [ \-\-json ]
.RI < " message"
.SH DESCRIPTION
Reads one message (RFC 5322) from standard input and delivers it to one
recipient, the way a local delivery agent does. This is the path an MTA
uses when it hands mail over locally, and the same path LMTP takes:
the recipient is resolved in the user database, the account's Sieve
script runs, and the message is stored where the script decides.
.PP
The server puts
.B Return-Path:
.RI < sender >
(\fB<>\fR for the null sender),
.B Delivered-To:
.I recipient
and its own
.B Received
field on top of the message, in that order, and removes a
.B Return-Path
the message brought along. The settings
.I server.lmtp_return_path
and
.I server.lmtp_delivered_to
switch the first two off.
.PP
The command prints where the message was stored, with mailbox and UID.
If Sieve discarded the message, nothing is stored and the result says so.
The same holds when a Sieve
.B reject
sent a rejection message to the sender: the message counts as delivered.
.PP
Role:
.B deliver
and, for
.BR \-\-no-enforce-quota ,
additionally
.BR deliver:noenforce .
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BI \-d " recipient"
Envelope recipient, as an address or an account. Required. An alias is
resolved to its canonical account.
.TP
.BI \-f " sender"
Envelope sender. Empty means the null sender, as for bounces.
.TP
.BI \-m " mailbox"
Store directly into this existing mailbox and do not run Sieve. Use it
when a message must land in a specific mailbox, for example while
re-injecting mail. A public folder is named the way a client sees it
.RI ( Public/Support ,
see
.IR server.public_prefix )
and needs the post right
.RB ( p )
for the recipient. There is no fallback: if the mailbox is missing the
command fails with 67, if the right is missing with 77. A mailbox of
another account is not a delivery target.
.TP
.B \-\-no-sieve
Do not run the user's Sieve script; the message goes to INBOX.
.TP
.B \-\-no-enforce-quota
Store even if the account is over its limit. The usage is still counted.
This is what a local delivery must do when a bounce is not acceptable.
.TP
.B \-\-json
Print the result as JSON.
.SH EXAMPLES
Deliver a file as if it had arrived by mail:
.PP
.nf
.RS
shardpostctl deliver -d user@example.org -f sender@example.net < mail.eml
.RE
.fi
.PP
Put a message back into a specific mailbox, without letting Sieve move it
again:
.PP
.nf
.RS
shardpostctl deliver -d user@example.org -m Archive < 933.eml
.RE
.fi
.PP
Re-inject a message that was exported earlier:
.PP
.nf
.RS
shardpostctl message export user@example.org INBOX 933 > 933.eml
shardpostctl deliver -d user@example.org --no-sieve < 933.eml
.RE
.fi
.PP
Deliver a system notice to an account that is over quota:
.PP
.nf
.RS
shardpostctl deliver -d user@example.org --no-enforce-quota < notice.eml
.RE
.fi
.PP
Check whether delivery works at all, end to end:
.PP
.nf
.RS
printf 'From: test@example.org\\nSubject: probe\\n\\nbody\\n' \\
    | shardpostctl deliver -d user@example.org --json
.RE
.fi
.SH EXIT STATUS
The codes follow
.IR sysexits.h ,
so an MTA can act on them:
.TP
.B 0
Delivered (or deliberately discarded by Sieve, or rejected by a Sieve
.B reject
that sent a rejection message to the sender).
.TP
.B 64
Usage error, or the server refused the request as malformed.
.TP
.B 67
No such recipient or mailbox.
.TP
.B 69
The message was rejected by a Sieve
.BR ereject ,
by a
.B reject
that could not send a rejection message (no relay, a relay that refused
it, or
.I sieve_reject_mode=protocol
in
.IR sieve.settings ),
or it is too large.
.TP
.B 70
The server sent an answer the client did not understand.
.TP
.B 73
Over quota.
.TP
.B 75
Temporary failure. The MTA should try again.
.TP
.B 77
The caller lacks the role, or the recipient may not post into the
mailbox named with
.BR \-m .
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR shardpostctl (1),
.BR shardpostctl-acl (1),
.BR shardpostctl-message (1),
.BR shardpostctl-sieve (1)
